    {
      "title": "Adjourned, not prorogued: Parliament Monsoon Session awaits formal closure",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/politics/adjourned-not-prorogued-parliament-monsoon-session-awaits-formal-closure-126082300174_1.html",
      "description": "Ten days after both Houses of Parliament were adjourned sine die, the Monsoon session is yet to end formally, giving rise to speculation that the government wants to pass a key legislation without convening a new session.\nBoth the Lok Sabha and the Rajya Sabha were adjourned sine die (for an indefinite period) on August 13. Usually, the session is prorogued (formally ended) within the next three to four days. However, the prorogation notification for the Monsoon session had not been issued as of Sunday morning.\nThe Budget session ended on April 18 and was prorogued on April 20.\nAccording to constitutional provisions, the president convenes and prorogues the session on the recommendation of the government.\nThere has been strong speculation that the government could bring the bill to amend the women's reservation law to ensure its implementation before the 2029 parliamentary polls.\nAn earlier attempt during the Budget session had failed as the government could not stack up the numbe",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-08-23T06:19:21"
    }
